  "body": {
    "id": "lem:two-sided-modulus",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{N,T,\\mathbf n}\\) and \\(0<\\delta\\le\\delta_0\\), \\[c_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\delta^{\\kappa}\\le\\omega_P(\\delta;z_0)\\le C\\delta^{\\kappa}.\\] The upper inequality follows from Holder smoothness and covariance ellipticity. The lower inequality is exactly the disclosed one-direction condition \\texttt{ass:attainable-local-modulus} and is not derived from the other panel conditions.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "M",
      "N",
      "T",
      "n_s",
      "n_vec",
      "delta_0",
      "c_mod",
      "kappa",
      "omega_P",
      "z_0",
      "C"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:holder-regression",
      "ass:donor-ellipticity",
      "ass:attainable-local-modulus",
      "def:panel-model-class",
      "def:local-modulus"
    ],
    "justification": "Holder smoothness and covariance ellipticity give the upper modulus, while the disclosed attainable direction supplies exactly the lower half.",
    "gap": "The lower direction is maintained and is not inferred from smoothness.",
    "consumer": "The donor two-point family converts this modulus into MSE and expected-length converses.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{N,T,\\mathbf n}\\), \\(0<\\delta\\le\\delta_0\\), and a\nfeasible \\(u=(u_1^{\\top},\\ldots,u_S^{\\top})^{\\top}\\) satisfying\n\\(\\|\\Sigma^{-1/2}u\\|_2\\le\\delta\\).  By\n\\texttt{ass:donor-ellipticity},\n\\[\n \\|u\\|_2^2\n =\\|\\Sigma^{1/2}\\Sigma^{-1/2}u\\|_2^2\n \\le\\lambda_{\\max}(\\Sigma)\\|\\Sigma^{-1/2}u\\|_2^2\n \\le C_\\Sigma\\delta^2.\n\\]\nLet \\(C_{\\mathrm{Hol}}\\) be the finite norm-equivalence constant determined\nby the stated isotropic Holder convention.  Assumption\n\\texttt{ass:holder-regression} implies\n\\[\n |m_s(x+v)-m_s(x)|\n \\le C_{\\mathrm{Hol}}L\\|v\\|_2^\\kappa,\n \\qquad \\kappa=\\min\\{\\lambda,1\\},\n\\]\nwhenever both arguments lie in \\([0,1]^d\\).  For\n\\(0<\\lambda\\le1\\) this is the defining Holder inequality.  For\n\\(\\lambda>1\\), the Holder-ball bound on the first derivatives and the\nfundamental theorem of calculus along the line segment from \\(x\\) to \\(x+v\\)\ngive the displayed Lipschitz inequality.\n\nSince \\(\\pi_s>0\\), \\(\\sum_s\\pi_s=1\\), and\n\\(\\|u_s\\|_2\\le\\|u\\|_2\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\left|\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\pi_s\n   \\{m_s(z_{0s}+u_s)-m_s(z_{0s})\\}\\right|\n &\\le C_{\\mathrm{Hol}}L\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\pi_s\\|u_s\\|_2^\\kappa\\\\\n &\\le C_{\\mathrm{Hol}}L C_\\Sigma^{\\kappa/2}\\delta^\\kappa.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over the feasible set in\n\\texttt{def:local-modulus} proves the upper inequality with\n\\(C=C_{\\mathrm{Hol}}LC_\\Sigma^{\\kappa/2}\\).  The lower inequality is\nexactly \\texttt{ass:attainable-local-modulus}.  Combining the two proves\n\\[\n c_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\delta^\\kappa\n \\le\\omega_P(\\delta;z_0)\n \\le C\\delta^\\kappa.\n\\]",
